<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Spain</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Spanish</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Spain is a country on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>. Parts of it were ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and Islamic states such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> before the Christian kingdoms completed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a>. United Spain built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>, conquering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a>, and competed with Portugal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/spain.jpg" alt="Spain" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antonio Valdés y Fernández Bazán · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Kingdom_of_Spain.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The intellectual output of the Abbasid Golden Age, recovered through Arabic translations in Sicily and <b>Spain</b>, helped lay the foundations of later European science.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 429 the Vandals and Alans under King Gaiseric crossed from <b>Spain</b> into Mauretania and moved east, defeating the Roman general Boniface and besieging Hippo Regius, where Augustine died in 430.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Alans were among the most widely travelled peoples of late antiquity, and their history connects the steppe, the Caucasus, Roman Gaul, <b>Spain</b> and North Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alans">Alans · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Almoravid Empire originated in one of history's most improbable transformations: a community of Saharan Berber nomads, galvanised by a reformist Islamic preacher, who within some seventy years came to rule a territory stretching roughly three thousand kilometres from the Senegal River to the Ebro in northern <b>Spain</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…changed completely: new roads, duty-free shopping, ski resorts and low taxes drew millions of visitors a year, banking grew, and the population rose from a few thousand in the 1940s to around 80,000 in the 2020s, a large share of them foreign nationals from <b>Spain</b>, Portugal and France.</blockquote><a href="/empire/andorra">Andorra · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Argentine Republic took its modern form after decades of conflict between Buenos Aires and the interior provinces that followed independence from <b>Spain</b>, declared at the Congress of Tucumán in 1816.</blockquote><a href="/empire/argentine-republic">Argentine Republic · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
